# Two-Axis Next-Generation Code Review: Tier 0 and L2 Verification Evidence
File: openOODA/audits/nextgen/axis/mcode3_evidence.oot

Provenance: converted from `openOODA/audits/nextgen-2axis-review-mcode3.md` (291 lines). Part 3 of 3.

Redaction note: fleet host names and tailnet addresses are written as generic
placeholders. The audited tree still holds the literals.

## 5. Verification method and negative-trust evidence

Every fix was checked with a positive and a negative case, as `rules.oot` §1.4 requires.

Table columns: Check | Positive | Negative
- D1, D2, D3, D4 | `make verify` and `make test` pass in all 15 L2 daemons, with the assertion count unchanged from the value on
  entry | The toy modulus is gone: `grep -rn 1000000007 <repo> --include='*.oo'` returns nothing. The four attestation rejection
  paths still fail closed with mask `3 / 6 / 5 / 4`, the zero-gas, over-budget, and non-positive-quota paths still fail closed,
  the audit tamper path still fails closed, and the quorum under-threshold, duplicate-badge, and split-brain paths still fail
  closed.
- D2 | The live capability gate returns true for the well-formed register | Each new assertion fails when its invariant is
  broken: a wrong epoch, an ungranted right, a widened child, a changed badge, or a changed rights field each drive the gate to
  `false`.
- D5 | The parity gate resolves the upstream program through `fs_path_exists` and reaches the same program the shell search
  reached (`/bin/ls` in the verification run) | With the capability jail active and `/usr/bin` outside the read set, the lookup
  returns empty and the gate fails closed with `ERR parity upstream tool ls is not installed.` — it never falls back to a shell.
- D6 | The ledger writes its epoch and Hawaii stamp from the canonical `TimeCap` | With no `OODA_FLEET_HOST_A` or
  `OODA_FLEET_HOST_B` in the environment, both fleet probes resolve to `127.0.0.1`, so a ledger written without configuration
  cannot reach a remote node.
- D8 | Every page of every L2 daemon now passes `oodac check` as a standalone root | The compiler rejects a page whose import is
  dangling, so the fix is observable: before it, `oodac check core/capreg.oo` failed with `unused import 'std/sec/quorum.oo'`;
  after it, the same command returns `OK`.
- D9 | The live attestation gate still yields mask `7` for a well-formed register and the delegation path still issues a child
  register | The canonical helper masks `req_rights = 256` down to *zero* rights and then passes. The fail-closed wrapper
  refuses it: `oo<prefix>_reg64_check(reg64, epoch, 256, 1)` returns `false` where `oo_cap_reg64_check(reg64, epoch, 256, 1)`
  returns `true`. Same for `oo<prefix>_reg64_pack(epoch, 256, badge, caps)`, which returns `0` where the canonical helper
  silently truncates the right to `0`.
- D10 | `oonetd`'s readiness probe still answers on both the primary and the fallback control port | The probe moved, not
  disappeared: `oonetd/net/readiness.oo` owns it and `main.oo` and `dns/cli.oo` call it through the new import. `core/capreg.oo`
  went from nine functions back to eight.
- D13 | The build compiles again after the page renames | Before the fix the build stopped with
  `make: *** No rule to make target 'verity/fs_verity.oo'`; after it, `make verify` and `make test` pass.

### 5.1 Sequential double-run determinism

Every L2 daemon's `make test` target already runs the binary twice and compares the two outputs byte for byte (`r1.txt` /
`r2.txt`, `cmp -s`), and the `det` target builds the daemon twice and compares the SHA-256 of the two binaries. Both are part of
the gate that was run for every remediation.

---

## 6. Environment notes

1. **The workspace runs concurrent review agents.** Three other reviewers were active during this review: `vibe-review 1` (L2
   OS, L4 DE, L6 Cloud, L7 Agents), `grok-review 1` (`oodar`, L1 Kernel, Win64 PE32+), and the `std` owner for `w3:p1`. Findings
   that belong to another owner's claim are reported here rather than fixed, so that no two agents write the same file.
2. **The `oodac` compiler wrapper enforces a 240-second timeout per invocation and a two-slot concurrency limit** (
   `/Projects/openOODA-core/oodac/bin/oodac`). Under this load a build that shares a repository with another agent serialises on
   the per-repo lock and can be killed with `Error 124`. The verification sweep therefore invokes `bin/oodac.real` directly and
   runs one build per repository directory. A `Killed` or `Error 124` line in this environment is a resource artefact, not a
   test failure.
3. **The canonical L0 tree was in motion throughout the review.** `std` gained `core/arena_mark.oo` and a rewired
   `sec/speculate.oo` at 17:47–17:56 HST while this review was running. The first baseline typecheck of `oollama` and `ooflash`
   failed against the broken `std/sec/speculate.oo`; the same command passed from 18:00 HST against the owner's fix. Both
   results are reported above rather than only the passing one.
4. **A second agent pushed overlapping work into the same repositories while this review was in flight.** Between 19:20 and
   21:45 HST a concurrent agent landed feature commits in all 15 L2 daemons and in `idlescreen`, including an alternative
   implementation of the same toy-modulus-to-canonical wiring (which kept a local copy of the 64-bit register and had no
   canonical OODA turn), page renames, and a raise of the `deep_e` assertion counts. Every push of this review's commits was
   therefore rejected as non-fast-forward until the commits were rebased onto the new remote heads. The resolution was: the
   concurrently pushed feature commits are kept, and this review's canonical implementation wins on the files the two overlap
   on, because it is the one that drops the copied register, keeps a fail-closed wrapper, runs the canonical 4-phase OODA turn,
   and binds the live gate to the canonical quorum and delegate helpers.
5. **One local branch was reset out from under this review.** At 21:43:56 HST `ooimage`'s `master` was reset to `origin/master`,
   discarding this review's three local commits; they were recovered from the reflog (`cf05624`) and replayed onto the new
   remote head as `d9cd2f0`, `799e08d`, `0a6dca4`. The fleet was re-checked afterwards by content rather than by git history —
   every daemon still carries the canonical OODA turn, the fail-closed wrappers, and zero toy modulus.
6. **The `Makefile` assertion gates were briefly stale.** Rebasing onto the concurrently pushed commits reverted four `Makefile`
   s to the pre-`deep_e` assertion counts, and the same commits left `SRC` naming pages that had been renamed. Both were
   repaired (D13) and the affected daemons were re-verified end to end before they were pushed.

---

## 7. Standards Axis — checklist against `rules.oot` §1.8

Table columns: Rung | Rule | Result
- `1` | One statement per line, zero symlinks and hardlinks | **PASS** — zero links of either kind in all 18 repositories
- `2` | Two test axes per repository, two-axis review | **PASS** — every L2 daemon runs a real functional I/O axis and a
  negative-trust refusal axis inside `make test`; the Tier 0 flagships were reviewed on both axes
- `4` | Four levels of block nesting | **PASS** — `lint-oo-size` reports no nesting violation
- `8` | Eight pages per directory, eight functions per page, eight parameters | **PASS** — `DENSE=0`, `MANYFN=0` in all 18
  repositories
- `16` | At least 16 lines per page, zero empty stubs | **PASS** — `line-cap` passes in every daemon
- `32` | Thirty-two explicit capabilities, 32-character names | **PASS** — every daemon maps 19 software and 13 hardware
  capabilities onto bits 0..31 with no dead bit, and the live gate asserts the full 32-bit coverage
- `64` | Sixty-four lines per function | **PASS** — `LONGFN=0` in all 18 repositories
- `128` | 128 characters per line | **PASS**
- `256` | 256 lines per page | **PASS** — `BIGFILE=0` in all 18 repositories, including the two parity pages that were over the
  ceiling until the shell-free lookup was compacted
